</div>
        <div><a name="_italian" id="_italian"></a><p class="h2Annex">C.22 Italian</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_c0e47044-6b6d-4874-84c6-189045d02b99" id="_c0e47044-6b6d-4874-84c6-189045d02b99"></a>A name in Italian consists of a given name (nome) and a surname (cognome).
Surnames are normally written after given names.</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_03ab4b9a-ab7b-45a3-96ae-5491c0813c22" id="_03ab4b9a-ab7b-45a3-96ae-5491c0813c22"></a>name components:</p>

  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo28;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpFirst">
    family name
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo28;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpLast">
    given name
  </p>
</div>
        <div><a name="_japanese" id="_japanese"></a><p class="h2Annex">C.23 Japanese</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_dc70a4a3-d263-4c64-b0a3-065e53c7b585" id="_dc70a4a3-d263-4c64-b0a3-065e53c7b585"></a>Japanese names usually consist of a family name (surname),
followed by a given name.</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_91e68ae4-81c9-4b5b-8a5f-1848ac3c1986" id="_91e68ae4-81c9-4b5b-8a5f-1848ac3c1986"></a>name components:</p>

  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo29;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpFirst">
    family name
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo29;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpLast">
    given name
  </p>

<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_e7c3556f-c176-44e8-818c-5e6d1028cc50" id="_e7c3556f-c176-44e8-818c-5e6d1028cc50"></a>Japanese names are usually written in kanji,
where characters are logographic, but each with a variety of
possible Japanese pronunciations.
Therefore, names often cannot be spelled or pronounced
unless both the spelling and pronunciation are given.</p>
<div><a name="_typical_name_patterns_7" id="_typical_name_patterns_7"></a><p class="h3Annex">C.23.1 Typical name patterns</p>

  <p class="TableTitle" align="center">Table C.33</p><div align="center"><table style="mso-table-lspace:15.0cm;margin-left:423.0pt;mso-table-rspace:15.0cm;margin-right:423.0pt;mso-table-bspace:14.2pt;mso-table-anchor-vertical:paragraph;mso-table-anchor-horizontal:column;mso-table-overlap:never;" class="MsoISOTable" border="1" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0"><a name="_b5d2fde1-9623-4548-9c54-859d0f075ff5" id="_b5d2fde1-9623-4548-9c54-859d0f075ff5"></a><tbody><tr><td align="left" style="border-top:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;">family name</td><td align="left" style="border-top:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-top-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;border-bottom:solid windowtext 1.5pt;mso-border-bottom-alt:solid windowtext 1.5pt;">given name</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
</div>
<div><a name="_examples_7" id="_examples_7"></a><p class="h3Annex">C.23.2 Examples</p><div class="example"><a name="_ad22cc0a-eea4-4df6-819c-3a47b202304d" id="_ad22cc0a-eea4-4df6-819c-3a47b202304d"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  1</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>&#x5927;&#x7FD4; is traditionally pronounced &#x201C;Hiroto&#x201D;, but can also be pronounced as
&#x201C;Haruto&#x201D;, &#x201C;Yamato&#x201D;, &#x201C;Taiga&#x201D;, &#x201C;Sora&#x201D;, &#x201C;Taito&#x201D;, &#x201C;Daito&#x201D;, and
&#x201C;Masato&#x201D;.</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_7fe182da-1404-4b0a-9787-151c462dea5e" id="_7fe182da-1404-4b0a-9787-151c462dea5e"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  2</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>&#x5927;&#x548C; can be read as Daiwa or Yamato.</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_45feed73-9509-408a-b265-9990917d1b54" id="_45feed73-9509-408a-b265-9990917d1b54"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  3</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>&#x6771;&#x6D77;&#x6797; can be read as T&#x14D;kairin or Sh&#x14D;ji.</p></div>
